{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    /*
     * THE SIBLING `paths` FROM tsconfig.json ARE DELIBERATELY DROPPED HERE, AND
     * DROPPING THEM IS WHAT MAKES THE PUBLISHED TYPES WORK.
     *
     * `vite-plugin-dts` emits declarations against THIS config. A `paths` mapping is
     * resolved before emit and the RESOLVED path is written into the output, so with
     * the sibling mappings inherited, three shipped declarations read
     *
     *     import { ColumnDef, SortState } from '../../../design-components/src/index.ts'
     *
     * — a path that escapes the published package, names a directory a consumer does
     * not have, points at `src/` which `design-components` does not publish, and
     * carries a `.ts` extension a consumer's `tsc` rejects. `DataTable`,
     * `DataTableRow` and `OperationsTablePage` were all affected. The emitted JS was
     * always correct (`from "@hollis-labs/design-components"`), so this broke types
     * only — nothing failed locally, and `tsc` said so 65 times in TS6059 while
     * `vite-plugin-dts` exited 0 anyway. Found and fixed by CW-20260910-0131 at
     * publish, before first release.
     *
     * Resolving through `node_modules` instead makes the emitted specifier the
     * package name, which is the only form that survives being published.
     *
     * WHY NOT FIX IT IN tsconfig.json: those mappings are load-bearing there and the
     * reasoning above them is right. `npm ci` does not build `design-components`
     * (it uses `prepack`, not `prepare`, on purpose), so typechecking a fresh clone
     * has to read sibling SOURCE or it reports TS2307. Typecheck wants source; emit
     * wants the package name. Two configs, two answers — and the split costs one
     * ordering requirement, stated next.
     *
     * WHAT THIS COSTS: building this package now requires its siblings' `dist` to
     * exist, so `npm run build -w @hollis-labs/kit-dashboard` on a never-built tree
     * fails with TS2307 rather than silently emitting broken declarations. The root
     * `npm run build` satisfies it — npm walks the workspaces in directory order and
     * every `design-*` package sorts before every `kit-*` one. That is an
     * alphabetical accident rather than a declared dependency order, and it is the
     * same accident `npm ci` already rests on; a package named before
     * `design-components` that depended on it would break both. Failing loudly is
     * the trade, and it is the right way round.
     */
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src"],
  "exclude": ["src/**/*.test.ts", "src/**/*.test.tsx", "src/__tests__"]
}
